entry alpha-particle
term: Alpha Particle (α)
image: ../../assets/images/terms/alpha particle.webp
related_terms: beta-particle
related_terms: gamma-ray
related_terms: decay
related_terms: radon
related_terms: ionizing-radiation
quick_facts: Identical to a helium-4 nucleus: 2 protons + 2 neutrons
quick_facts: Stopped by a sheet of paper, skin, or a few centimeters of air
quick_facts: Cause dense ionization along their very short path
quick_facts: Most dangerous when inhaled or swallowed — e.g. radon gas in lungs
quick_facts: Emitted by heavy elements like uranium, radium, and radon

sources:
    {"organization":"EPA","title":"Radiation Basics","url":"https://www.epa.gov/radiation/radiation-basics"}

definition:
    A heavy, positively charged particle made of 2 protons and 2 neutrons — identical to a helium-4 nucleus — emitted by heavy, unstable nuclei such as uranium, radium, and radon during alpha decay. Alpha particles barely penetrate matter: paper or the outer layer of skin stops them. But they ionize intensely along their short path, making them among the most damaging radiations when alpha emitters are inhaled or ingested.

debunking_snippet:
    “Alpha radiation is harmless because it can't get through skin.” Outside the body, mostly true — but one alpha particle inside the lungs tears through countless cells. That's why inhaled radon is a leading cause of lung cancer among non-smokers.
